WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.120
SPEAKER 1: So they
give you the picture

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.100
of how this data
platform puts together,

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.980
we're going to use a scenario
of an advertising agency

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.860
that puts ads in the back of
those screens in New York City

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:09.430
taxis.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.310
If ever if you've been in
a cab recently in New York.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.871
There's an ad that plays,
and you can interact with it.

00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:16.329
To give you the
picture first, this

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:19.930
is a simplified architecture
of this data solution.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.240
The taxis are sending their
position in real time.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.920
Where they are in
the city, as well

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.590
as their destination, how
many passengers they have,

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.710
and what sort of interactions
the customers are doing.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.720
Pub/Sub is then
ingesting that data.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.150
We're using dataflow to
process a data pipeline.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.730
To handle and process
all that data.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.280
We're also archiving the
entire historical data

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.220
in BigQuery, which is a
really powerful tool for you

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.980
to do ad hoc analysis of
all of your historical data.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.120
And at the end we want
to make a visualization.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.530
So why don't we actually
see the pipeline in action.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.481
So this the dataflow
visualization tool.

00:00:56.481 --> 00:00:57.980
And you see Robert's
hovered-- we're

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.450
ingesting a little over
15,000 new elements per second

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.390
into this database, of
all of this information.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.400
But how do you
visualize all that?

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.170
Google Maps is super
powerful, but I'd

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.820
hate to point 15,000 updates
per second at my Chrome browser.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.880
So dataflow actually allows
us to down-sample that events

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.880
stream to give us a real
time visualization of taxi

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:20.600
positions.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.220
And this is that visualization.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.650
This is showing where all
these taxis are in New York

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.000
City at any given time.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.690
Now what if I want to look at
one particular customer who's

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:30.660
placed ads with us.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.090
A particular agency.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.900
We can filter that dataflow,
and now the visualization

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.960
will update so that we're
only seeing ads that are being

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.429
placed for a specific store.

00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:42.470
In this case, the Acme
store-- it looks like it's

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.397
on the Upper East Side.

00:01:44.397 --> 00:01:46.730
And sure enough, we see that
we're placing ads, roughly,

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.190
geographically, near the store.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.942
But right now, we're just
using a dumb rule for this.

00:01:51.942 --> 00:01:53.150
It's just a very simple rule.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:53.810
Where is the store?

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:54.560
Where's the taxi?

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.000
Place the ads.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.570
Can we do better?

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.150
Can we automate this
to start to take

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:03.260
into account all the various
data that we have about this?

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.240
Well, let's start
by using BigQuery

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.910
to explore this dataset.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:12.440
The query Robert has here looks
at all of the taxis coming

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.050
from the airports and tries
to see how many of them

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.390
are traveling near restaurants.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.850
Now note here he's querying
this historical data,

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.966
even as we're ingesting new data
at 15,000 events per second.

00:02:23.966 --> 00:02:25.340
So BigQuery gives
him the ability

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.090
to have an always up-to-date
real time view into his data.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.230
In this case, we see that
roughly 17% of our taxis

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.712
are from the airport
and near a restaurant.

00:02:35.712 --> 00:02:37.670
That sounds like an
insight that maybe we ought

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.835
to do some ad targeting there.

00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:41.210
And if you look
at his query, you

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.460
can see it really is
a non-trivial query.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.370
And BigQuery is handling
all that for us.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:51.440
So right now, we are manually
configuring these ads.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.830
So I think what we want
to do is look at a way

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:58.590
that we can do this in
an automatic fashion.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.620
Now we could similarly search
for every different customer,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.840
but how do we build a
generalizable and flexible

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.636
model.

00:03:04.636 --> 00:03:06.760
Well that sounds like a
job for a machine learning.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.509
Machine learning is
tailor-made for taking

00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:10.590
large chunks of data
and building models

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.460
that give you
insights out of that.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.970
So we're going to build a model
that takes into account all

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:15.830
of the data we have.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:16.902
Where did the taxi start?

00:03:16.902 --> 00:03:17.610
Where did it end?

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.734
How many people were in it?

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:19.710
What time of day is it?

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.030
Where's it going?

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.654
First, we're going
to start by using--

00:03:23.654 --> 00:03:24.820
as you see in this picture--

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.140
we're going to use Dataprep.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.060
To take all that
historical data,

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.504
clean it up, make sure
that it's ready to use,

00:03:30.504 --> 00:03:32.420
and then we're going to
use it with ML engine.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.000
So this is Dataprep.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.040
The first thing to
notice is across the top,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.280
Dataprep has automatically
inferred the schema

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.090
from my table.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.010
I didn't have to tell it that.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.660
Now if you look at that
passenger count column,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.990
you'll notice that it
looks a little funny.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:51.010
The histogram, up at the top,
includes some elements that

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.519
says there's 10 or 11 people.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:55.060
Now, I don't know about you,
but I've been in a New York City

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.010
cab, and I'm pretty
sure you can't actually

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.140
put 10 or 11 people in a cab.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.420
So that's a common data bug.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.600
Dataprep allows me to fix
that in a number of ways.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:05.950
I could delete the data.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.860
I could replace that 11
with a one, on the theory

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.720
that it's a fat-finger mistake.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.840
And Dataprep allows me to build
simple recipes that clean up

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.360
my entire data at scale.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.089
It also can do things like-- if
you look at our location column

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:20.410
there, it's a composite column.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.200
It's longitude and
latitude, comma separated.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.200
I'd really like to have a
latitude column and a longitude

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.160
column.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.990
Dataprep allows me to
build a recipe that

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.390
splits columns with Composite
data into separate columns.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:35.770
So that I now have a
better schema to work with.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.330
Now this just
scratches the surface

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.630
of how Dataprep can take this
really tedious job of preparing

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.510
data for analytics, and make
it much faster and much more

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.040
reliable.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.840
Once we've done this,
machine learning,

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.550
as you heard from
[INAUDIBLE], allows

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.940
me to build a model on my
laptop, upload it to Google,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.590
and deploy at scale.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.130
Let's see how that
actually works.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.520
If you scroll down in
the dataflow pipeline,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.160
you'll see we've added
an ML engine call.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.860
So now, as our taxi
data is coming in,

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.930
we're calling to machine
learning with the data

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.500
and saying, help us
choose the best ad

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.890
to target for this
particular user.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.010
So let's switch
back over to the map

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.600
with the deployed
machine learning model,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.600
and now you'll see
that we start seeing

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.220
some interesting combinations.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.400
When there's one passenger
coming in towards midtown--

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.250
the taxi is currently in
Chelsea, and it's on its way

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.112
to midtown--

00:05:25.112 --> 00:05:27.070
we're presenting an offer
for Manhattan Bagels.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.080
Because it's a single
person, maybe he or she

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.790
wants a bite to eat.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:34.820
If we pick a taxi that has
more than one person in it--

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.770
There it is.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:47.170
In this case, that's
a one person one,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.080
but they're heading into
a different location.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.940
And instead we've
chosen a winery.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.772
If you pick a taxi
with three people,

00:05:53.772 --> 00:05:56.230
the system would automatically
recommend discounted tickets

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.490
to Broadway, for example--

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.030
as multiple people coming
to midtown in the evening.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.610
So that's Cloud ML,
making suggestions based

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:06.040
on all our available data.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:10.450
We used Pub/Sub to ingest a
huge stream of data at scale.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.520
We use dataflow to build
a pipeline to give us

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.980
a good analytics system.

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.750
We used BigQuery
so that we always

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:19.060
had the complete
historical data available.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.580
We never had to deal
with sample data.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.820
And at the end, we use Cloud ML
and Dataprep to build and train

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:26.350
at scale a model.

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.330
What's important here
is what you didn't see.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.130
I didn't deploy a
virtual machine.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.320
I didn't deploy patches.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.290
I spent my time
actually in the data.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.030
That's the point of big data is
to spend the time in the data

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.700
and not in the machines
that take care of the data.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.952
And that's what big data on
the Google platform does.

00:06:42.952 --> 00:06:45.160
So I want to thank you all
and I'll turn it back over

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.770
to Brian.

